WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.170
YouTube

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.900
We live in a world of overused hyperbole.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.340
Still it is hard to overstate the phenomenon
that is YouTube.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:12.400
In just over a decade, it has risen from nothing
to 7 billion hours of video watched every

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.400
month.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.680
It is the third most visited site on the internet,
with 80 percent of viewers coming from outside

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.680
the United States.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:24.859
It has more than 1.5 billion monthly users,
who are entertained by 400 hours worth of

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:28.050
video uploaded every 60 seconds.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.960
And it has made a lot of people a whole lot
of money…

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.079
But have you ever stopped and wondered how
YouTube came about?

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:38.329
It’s a fascinating story and today, you
are going to discover it all as Biographics

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:45.749
goes behind the screen to bring you the real
YouTube story.

00:00:45.749 --> 00:00:47.280
The Idea

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.749
It all started with a chance encounter in
1999.

00:00:52.749 --> 00:00:57.379
Eric Skaggs, a local entrepreneur, was finishing
up some work on his website at the Eberly

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:01.659
College of Business computer lab at Indiana
University of Pennsylvania.

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:06.509
His website was called GoGetItNow.com, and
was an amalgamation of email, search engine

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:10.100
and web hosting combined into a single interface.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.090
As he was about to leave the building, Skaggs
saw a rather tall guy in a baseball camp entering.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.149
They struck up a conversation and Skaggs learnt
that this guy, Chad Hurley, was a graphics

00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:22.399
arts major who was looking for an idea for
a website.

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:28.070
The idea that Skaggs gave to Hurley was to
provide the inspiration for the YouTube revolution...

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.960
He told him that in the future television
and computers would become fully integrated.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.950
Furthermore people would be able to use their
cell phones to create home videos and they

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.049
would want a platform to share them with friends
and family.

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:43.970
Remember, this was at a time when there were
no cameras with video in phones and TV and

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.450
the internet were very different animals.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.359
The idea was a little overwhelming for Hurley
and he stored it away for future reference.

00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:55.140
After graduating from Indiana University with
a fine arts degree he started looking for

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.070
employment in the exciting new internet industry.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.680
He picked up a copy of Wired magazine which
had an article featuring the Top 10 website

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.680
companies from his local grocery store.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.930
Hurley sat down and redesigned those companies
logos, sending them to the marketing departments.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:14.840
The one he did for a company called Paypal
caught the eye of co-founder Eion Musk and,

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.580
before he knew it, Hurley was hired as the
company’s first graphic designer.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.819
PayPal

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:27.140
It was at PayPal that Hurley was able to pick
the brains of the most brilliant techie nerds

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.140
in the industry.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.710
The two smartest guys in the room were Steve
Chen and Jawed Karim.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:36.989
Chen was born in Taiwan in 1978, with his
family moving to the United States when he

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.130
was eight years old.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:42.940
He attended the Illinois Mathematics and Science
Academy where he proved to be a maths prodigy.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.629
His passion was computer engineering, and
he went on to study computer science at the

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:48.120
University of Illinois.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.390
He began working for Paypal in 1999, a few
months before Hurley.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:57.290
Jawed Karim was born in Merseburg, Germany
in 1979, moving with his family to the U.S.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:58.290
when he was thirteen.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.780
From the start, Jawed was a computer wiz.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.280
In school he wrote computer programs, posting
them on the internet for free.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.000
He also helped to set up the schools’ e-mail
system.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.200
Just like Steve Chen, Karim went to the University
of Illinois, where he studied computer science.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.410
After a number of internships with a range
of technology companies, he sought employment

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.940
with Paypal in 2000, where he was put to work
on designing the company’s anti-fraud security

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.940
system.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.689
Hurley, the graphic designer, Chen, the engineer
and Karim, the systems guru, soon found each

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:29.770
other and spent more and more time brainstorming
ideas for their own start-up.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:34.140
For five years, they kicked around ideas while
building up their knowledge and experience

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.140
at Paypal.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.099
Then, in January of 2005, Hurley was hosting
a dinner party at his home.

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:44.150
Chen was in attendance and he began using
a camcorder to take some video of the festivities.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.700
The next day they wanted to share the video
with those who had been in attendance.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.220
The problem was that the video files were
way too big to send by email.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.099
And, even though there were a few photo sharing
websites, such as Flickr and Shutterfly, nothing

00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:58.780
existed for the sharing of video online.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.719
It was at this point that the seed that had
been planted years before came to life.

00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:06.800
Teaming with Karim, they decided to create
a website to share videos.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.750
The idea was to use the video sharing platform
to create a dating site.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.140
People would post self videos for potential
partners to view and respond to if they liked

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:15.140
what they saw.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.700
They called it ‘Tune In, Hook Up.’

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.960
They launched the site within days and then
waited for the users to come.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.160
However, nothing happened.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.430
In an attempt to get some content up on the
site, they placed ads on Craigslist, offering

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.160
to pay people to post videos.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.120
Still, no one was interested and ‘Tune In,
Hook Up’ became a huge flop.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.110
The trio were disheartened but not dismayed.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:40.250
Shortly after the demise of their first joint
venture, Jawed was frustratingly searching

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:46.940
online for a video of Janet Jackson’s infamous
2004 Superbowl half-time show wardrobe malfunction.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.860
Because there was no user friendly platform
for sharing video content, he was unable to

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.820
satisfy his breast fetish.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.110
Together with Hurley and Chen, he decided
to do something about it.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:00.520
Stripping away the lame dating concept, they
were left with a video uploading and sharing

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.520
platform.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.520
All they need now was a new name for their
website.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.170
Founding

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:13.420
On February 14th, 2005, in a humble apartment
above a California pizza shop, the trio began

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.070
building their new website.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:19.170
Initially, apparently in reference to Janet
Jackson’s unwitting contribution, they wanted

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.720
to call it BoobTube.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.310
Realizing that that sounded too much like
a porn site, they began playing around with

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.500
words that conveyed the idea of personalized
television.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:31.430
Harking back to the days when the TV was called
‘the tube’ thanks to the cathode ray tube

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.970
that powered it, one of the three (no one
knows which one) came up with YouTube.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:40.380
It sounded perfect - it was short, catchy
and fun and it perfectly summed up what they

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.540
were about.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.750
When Hurly applied to register the domain
name, the idea was to go with Utube with a

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:47.850
‘U’.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.741
However that domain name was already taken,
which forced him to precede ‘Tube’ with

00:05:51.741 --> 00:05:53.320
the three letter word “You.’

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:57.750
Hurly, Chen and Karim immediately set about
writing down the goals for YouTube.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:01.160
At the top of the list was ease of user experience.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.520
Loading and watching videos had to be super
easy, so that even the most computer illiterate

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:06.770
person could do it.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.370
They then divided up the workload.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:12.330
Chad Hurly was in charge of the look and design
of the site as well tagging and labelling

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.300
loaded videos to make them easy for viewers
to find.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.050
Steve Chen was the engineer who made sure
that the site was running smoothly and that

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.660
videos played without lagging.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.800
Jawid Karim, meanwhile, used his programming
skills to get the site up and running and

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.050
looking great.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.680
Unlike the other two, Karim chose not to be
an employee of the company.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.690
Instead his role was that of an informal advisor.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:37.420
This allowed him to focus on his graduate
studies in computer science at Stanford University.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:41.770
The first video ever loaded onto YouTube was
an eighteen second long clip of Karim at the

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.650
San Diego zoo entitled ‘Me at the Zoo.’

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:48.470
You can still watch this historic clip on
the site, where it has been seen by more than

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:49.930
30 million people.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:51.860
Hurly was put in charge of marketing.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:55.930
He started a competition where a different
YouTube user was given an Apple Ipod Nano

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:58.140
every day for 8 weeks.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.780
People earned points by referring a friend
or posting a video.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.150
The more points you gained, the greater your
chances of winning the Ipod.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.650
The promotion worked and people began flooding
the site with video content.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.110
However, this created a problem.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.490
So much content was coming onboard that YouTube
was running out of bandwidth.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.760
This made video playback excruciatingly slow.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.870
They were desperately in need of more storage
capacity, which required money.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.650
But, as of yet, they weren’t making any.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:29.570
Fortunately, on the basis of future ad revenue
forecasts, they were able to secure a $3.5

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:33.510
million dollar venture capital loan in November,
2005.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:37.970
Just a month earlier a Nike promotional video
featuring Brazilian football star, Ronaldhino,

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:42.660
became the first Youtube clip to reach one
million views.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.370
Explosive Growth

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:49.750
By the beginning of 2006, YouTube had grown
to more than 60 employees and the site had

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.830
more than 100 million videos.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:55.930
This was an astounding level of growth, considering
that just 12 months earlier they literally

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:59.370
couldn’t pay people to post videos on their
website.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:03.300
Over the course of its first year the nature
of the content being loaded on YouTube evolved.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.520
During the first few months the majority of
clips were of the home movie variety.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.270
But then more and more ‘How To’ videos
began appearing, with advice being offered

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.300
on everything from how to change the oil in
your car to the proper technique for doing

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.380
a barbell squat.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.810
As a result, YouTube was fast becoming the
go to source for both entertainment and information.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:26.050
In June, 2006 YouTube entered into a contract
with NBC television.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:30.780
This marked a u-turn by NBC, who had previously
forced YouTube to remove a Saturday Night

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:33.479
live clip after it became a viral hit online.

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:39.690
Now NBC agreed to pay YouTube to place promotional
clips of their shows, behind the scenes interviews

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:43.169
and other features on the website.

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:44.949
Google Buyout

00:08:44.949 --> 00:08:49.420
YouTube was by now attracting the attention
of the biggest players in the Web universe.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:54.100
The site was wildly popular with viral videos
regularly appearing on TV news broadcasts,

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.439
and even entire shows being devoted to YouTube
content.

00:08:57.439 --> 00:09:02.209
The sort of global reach that YouTube had
acquired was extremely inviting to the biggest

00:09:02.209 --> 00:09:03.560
player of them all.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:10.040
In October, 2006, the Google corporation purchased
YouTube for a staggering 1.65 billion dollars.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:15.189
The three tech nerds from Paypal now had more
money than they could possibly spend in a

00:09:15.189 --> 00:09:16.189
lifetime.

00:09:16.189 --> 00:09:20.360
Despite being independently wealthy beyond
measure, Hurley and Chen stayed with YouTube.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.740
In fact, they were thrilled to be working
with such a Web giant as Google.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.630
They now had the freedom to focus on adding
new technology to the website.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:31.940
In an effort to encourage people to stay on
the site for longer, they added extra features,

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:36.250
including suggestions of follow up videos
the viewer may be interested in watching.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.570
They also set to work on making the YouTube
experience available through a greater range

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:41.570
of devices.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.070
YouTube’s mobile app let people watch videos
on their cell phones and people could now

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:47.779
add YouTube clips to their websites and blogs.

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:52.610
In a move that emphasized just how dominant
YouTube was in people’s lives, Time magazine

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:58.300
featured a YouTube Screen on the cover of
its 2006 Person of the year issue.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.360
Paying Creators

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.589
In May, 2007, the YouTube Partner Program
was launched.

00:10:04.589 --> 00:10:09.170
This allowed people who uploaded videos to
have a share in the ad revenue generated by

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:11.390
advertising that was played during the video.

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:15.589
Now, for the first time, every day people
were able to turn their hobbies into successful

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:16.980
online businesses.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:22.759
The program, which is still in operation,
pays uploaders around 55% of the ad revenue.

00:10:22.759 --> 00:10:27.170
Over the last decade this revenue source has
paid off big time for a relatively small number

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:32.449
of creators, known as YouTubers, who have
created massive followings and huge annual

00:10:32.449 --> 00:10:33.449
earnings.

00:10:33.449 --> 00:10:37.170
They have also been able to leverage their
popularity to obtain sponsorship deals with

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:40.819
merchandisers who are keen to get their products
in the videos.

00:10:40.819 --> 00:10:47.639
Some of these YouTubers have even landed movie
and TV roles as a result of their online fame.

00:10:47.639 --> 00:10:49.499
Impact

00:10:49.499 --> 00:10:54.300
2007 was also the year that YouTube began
impacting politics.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:59.509
Seven of the thirteen candidates for the Presidency
of the United States announced their campaigns

00:10:59.509 --> 00:11:00.699
through YouTube.

00:11:00.699 --> 00:11:04.950
This was followed by the first YouTube presidential
debate which featured questions submitted

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:05.950
by viewers.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:11.199
In 2009, the capability was introduced to
upload and watch 3-D videos on YouTube.

00:11:11.199 --> 00:11:15.779
That same year the staggering milestone of
one billion views per day was reached.

00:11:15.779 --> 00:11:19.959
Less than three years later that number had
mushroomed to 4 billion.

00:11:19.959 --> 00:11:24.089
The first video to reach one billion views
was the unforgettable ‘Gangnam Style’

00:11:24.089 --> 00:11:27.980
by PSY which reached the milestone on December
21, 2012.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:31.629
In 2011, the new feature of livestreaming
was added.

00:11:31.629 --> 00:11:36.100
Now major world events could be watched in
real time, making YouTube a viable alternative

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.579
to the television set.

00:11:37.579 --> 00:11:42.889
But YouTube was soon to prove that it was
more than just a recorder of historical events.

00:11:42.889 --> 00:11:48.259
January 25th, 2011 was a day of protest against
the government of President Hosni Mubarak

00:11:48.259 --> 00:11:49.370
in Cairo, Egypt.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:53.839
Throughout that day video of the proclaimed
‘day of rage’ began appearing on YouTube.

00:11:53.839 --> 00:11:58.819
This inspired and emboldened thousands of
Egyptian citizens who came pouring out into

00:11:58.819 --> 00:11:59.819
the streets.

00:11:59.819 --> 00:12:04.149
The protests went on for 18 days and the protesters
were able to use YouTube, along with Facebook

00:12:04.149 --> 00:12:08.379
and Twitter, to galvanize the support that
would eventually lead to change.

00:12:08.379 --> 00:12:12.769
Mubarak’s regime reacted to the skilful
use of the internet on the part of its detractors

00:12:12.769 --> 00:12:15.939
by imposing a sweeping crackdown of the internet.

00:12:15.939 --> 00:12:20.399
This only further enraged the protesters,
eventually forcing Mubarak to step down and

00:12:20.399 --> 00:12:24.440
democratic elections to be held for the first
time in decades.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.779
2011 also saw a major overhaul of the website’s
look.

00:12:27.779 --> 00:12:32.740
The video channels were displayed in a central
column and the YouTube logo was updated.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:37.180
The Google Chrome web browser was also integrated
so that viewers could watch YouTube directly

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:38.180
watched through Google+.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.529
Two years later, YouTube began offering paid
subscriptions.

00:12:41.529 --> 00:12:46.959
For a monthly fee, this service allowed subscribers
to watch longer, ad free videos and movies.

00:12:46.959 --> 00:12:49.379
Where are They Now?

00:12:49.379 --> 00:12:53.059
So, what are the founders of YouTube up to
now?

00:12:53.059 --> 00:12:58.190
In October of 2010, Chad Hurley stepped down
as CEO of YouTube, handing the reins over

00:12:58.190 --> 00:12:59.190
to Salar Kamangar.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.050
He committed to staying on as an advisor to
the company.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:06.019
Steve Chen left the company at about the same
time and the two began collaborating on a

00:13:06.019 --> 00:13:07.730
new venture, AVOS Systems.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:12.769
Their headline product was an iPhone app called
MixBit, which allows users to shoot, edit

00:13:12.769 --> 00:13:14.019
and share video clips.

00:13:14.019 --> 00:13:18.970
AVOS systems has acquired a number of other
companies, including Delicious and Tap 11.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:22.999
Chad Hurley was also a major sponsor of the
USF1 Formula One racing team.

00:13:22.999 --> 00:13:28.759
He is also a part owner of NBA team the Golden
State Warriors and the MLS Los Angeles Football

00:13:28.759 --> 00:13:29.759
Club.

00:13:29.759 --> 00:13:33.899
In 2014, after fifteen of years of collaboration,
Chen and Hurley parted company.

00:13:33.899 --> 00:13:38.829
Chad took over full control of MixBit, while
Chen went over to Google Ventures as an entrepreneur

00:13:38.829 --> 00:13:40.100
in residence.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:43.050
Making it clear that there was no animosity
in the parting, Chen explained . . .

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:47.889
Actually, it was a very personal and difficult
decision to make, but with the decision to

00:13:47.889 --> 00:13:49.949
turn Avos into MixBit it became pretty clear.

00:13:49.949 --> 00:13:53.939
MixBit was Chad’s idea before we started
Avos and even at YouTube, and if I feel driven

00:13:53.939 --> 00:13:58.079
to work on other projects, then I’m just
getting in the way of mixbit and the team.

00:13:58.079 --> 00:14:01.999
Jawed Karim completed his graduate studies
at the University of Illinois, emerging with

00:14:01.999 --> 00:14:03.589
a Master’s degree in Computer Science.

00:14:03.589 --> 00:14:07.070
Of the three founders, he received the lowest
portion of stock.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:11.670
However, this was still enough for him to
earn around $64 million from the sale to Google.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:16.749
In 2008, he launched Youniversity Ventures,
which provides mentorship, expertise and contacts

00:14:16.749 --> 00:14:20.240
for college students to take innovative products
to the next level.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.619
Karim was also one of the first investors
in Airbnb in 2009.

00:14:24.619 --> 00:14:29.720
Towards the end of 2013, Karim hit the headlines
when he objected, along with many other YouTube

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:34.370
users, to Google’s requirement that commenting
on YouTube videos only be done through a Google

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:35.370
+ account.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.009
He showed his displeasure by changing the
description on his iconic ‘Me at the zoo’

00:14:39.009 --> 00:14:40.009
video to read . . .

00:14:40.009 --> 00:14:43.329
I can’t comment here anymore since I don’t
want a google+ account.

00:14:43.329 --> 00:14:49.370
As a result of Karim’s stance, backed up
by a 240,000 signature petition, Google dropped

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:54.800
its requirement that commenters must use their
Google + product.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.529
The Future

00:14:57.529 --> 00:15:02.259
It is truly mind-blowing to contemplate just
how big YouTube will become in the next few

00:15:02.259 --> 00:15:03.259
years.

00:15:03.259 --> 00:15:06.709
New features are constantly being rolled out
to enhance the viewer experience.

00:15:06.709 --> 00:15:10.660
YouTube has already replaced TV as the main
source of entertainment and information for

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:11.660
many people.

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:15.430
According to Google Executive chairman Eric
Schmidt, the exponential growth of YouTube

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:20.149
will bring it six or seven billion unique
visitors per month, with that growth being

00:15:20.149 --> 00:15:22.970
largely driven from the developing world.

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:27.220
YouTube has became a launching pad for a growing
number of breakout stars, including Justin

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.970
Bieber and Greyson Chance.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:33.600
There have also been a number of TV shows
that began life as skits on YouTube, including

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.170
Broad City and Workaholics.

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:40.899
YouTube is the most public place in the world,
accessible from the privacy of our bedrooms.

00:15:40.899 --> 00:15:45.660
It has created unprecedented opportunities
for learning, entertaining and earning a living.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.700
But, just like anything, YouTube can be abused.

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:51.220
For one thing it can be mind numbingly addictive.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:55.160
You sit down to watch a 3 minute clip and
three hours later you’re still slouched

00:15:55.160 --> 00:16:07.819
in front of the computer watching cats playing

00:16:07.819 --> 00:16:23.870
the piano, or hopefully, you know, watching
a Biographics video!

